text:
maria: quick tf question — trying to lock a KMS key so only pods from a specific serviceaccount can decrypt. used a condition on the key policy with aws:SourceIdentity but decrypt still failing from the pod. any pointers?

maria: simplified snippet i tested:```json
{
  "Sid": "AllowIRSADecrypt",
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/eks-irsa-role"},
  "Action": ["kms:Decrypt"],
  "Resource": "*",
  "Condition": {
    "StringEquals": {"aws:SourceIdentity": "system:serviceaccount:payments/backend"}
  }
}
```

maria: TF iam role is standard IRSA assume role (oidc/something), and the pod has annotation `eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn` on the sa. decrypts as cluster job if i remove the Condition.

jon: hmm — aws:SourceIdentity is set by the caller when AssumeRole is used with SourceIdentity param. I don't think the OIDC -> STS flow for IRSA sets SourceIdentity automatically.

jon: two common patterns: 1) restrict by role ARN (simple, coarse), or 2) restrict by session tags/principal tags if you can ensure tags are passed into the session. IRSA supports passing requested tags via the token if role's trust policy allows `sts:TagSession` — but you need the token exchange/assume call to include those tags.

jon: if you want per-serviceaccount granularity, using `aws:PrincipalTag` in the KMS key policy is usually more reliable. Example: add session tag `sa` at assume-time and then:`Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/sa": "payments-backend"}}`

maria: ok, how do we ensure the session gets that tag with IRSA? i thought IRSA was just assume-with-web-identity and limited in what it can pass.

kevin: you can use the IAM Role's `sts:TagSession` permission and include `aws:RequestTag` in the role trust policy to allow the OIDC provider to pass tags. In TF that looks like allowing `sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity` with `Condition` allowing `StringEquals` for `token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub` style claim, plus `StringEquals` for `sts:TagSession`? (high level).

kevin: e.g. trust policy fragment:```json
"Condition": {
  "StringEquals": {
    "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub": "repo:..."
  },
  "ForAnyValue:StringLike": {
    "aws:RequestTag/sa": "payments-backend"
  }
}
```

kevin: then when your pod exchanges the token, the STS call needs to pass the tags. I know the EKS projected token flow supports `audience` and subject, but passing tags is on the assume call side — certain kube controllers or the aws sdk can do it, or you'd need the IRSA shim you control to call AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity and add the tags.

maria: ahh ok so default IRSA via kube doesn't add arbitrary tags — biting point. thanks.

jon: TL;DR options: 1) Accept coarser control: restrict by role ARN in key policy (fast). 2) Use dedicated key per service (simpler for compliance). 3) Invest in session-tagging flow so KMS policy can check `aws:PrincipalTag/*` or `aws:SourceIdentity`.

maria: for our short-term goal (payments staging) i'll go with role-arn restriction + note to design per-service keys later. cc @kevin i'll follow up for a follow-up ticket for session-tagging PoC.

infra-bot: PSA: docs on our IRSA patterns + KMS key policy templates -> https://internal.redwood/docs/infra/irsa-kms-patterns (have the role-arn and per-namespace key templates) :eyes:

jon: also added a short PR comment on your TF to suggest key alias naming convention. :+1:
